> Anyway, IMO Spork is slightly easier early on and somewhat harder in
> the late game than vanilla.  

If this is the way most players perceive it, I'm OK with that.  There
was an explicit goal to make the early game no harder (though it seems I
may have some leeway to add a few things there now), in the interest of
not alienating newer players with new, exciting "You die on DL1!"
messages.

I need to focus more on the midgame at the moment; I think the late-game
is about as 'hard' as it can be without adding more explicit anti-player
mechanisms, which generally lack style.  It would be good now if the
midgame were a bit more interesting than just "slog to the Castle, get
the wand, slog through to Vlad's", and ideally it should actually be a
_challenging_ slog, rather than what it tends to be ATM.

> I don't personally like the overall
> design goals (removal of binary resistances, adding more beankeeping)

Which parts are you thinking of as "beankeeping"?  

(The binary resistances we are just going to disagree on, of course; the 
best example I can give of a still-extant problem is fire elementals, who 
are completely harmless to a fire-resistant character.  This strikes me 
as odd given that they're all over the Plane of Fire and clearly supposed 
to be one of the major obstacles to passing that plane.  I don't have a 
good solution for that yet, though, and I'm not willing to take the approach 
of "no intrinsic 100% resistance" just yet; that is a much bigger change than
it appears, because so much of the game was designed around the player
likely _having_ 100% resistance.)

